# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the leak lane (scripts/sanitize.sh lsan).
#
# THE RULE FOR THIS FILE: an entry may only describe an allocation that is
#   (a) made inside a library we do not own and did not ask for directly, and
#   (b) one-time or bounded — something the library never frees by design, not
#       something that grows with the work the test does,
# and every entry names the run that proved it and what the leak actually was.
# A suppression that hides one of OUR allocations makes the whole lane
# worthless; if a leak is ours the lane is doing its job — fix it, or record it
# as a finding. Suppressing anything under src/ or irisgl/ is forbidden.
#
# The lane runs with print_suppressions=1, so every run prints how many bytes
# each of these actually matched. A line that stops matching is deleted (an
# unmatched suppression is a claim nobody is checking any more); a line whose
# byte count starts growing is no longer one-time and has to be re-audited.
#
# Format: leak:<substring of a function name, source file or library>, matched
# against every frame of the allocation stack.
# https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AddressSanitizer.html#suppressing-memory-leaks

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# libdbus-1 — 184 bytes, one allocation, in the suites that construct a
# QGuiApplication with Qt Multimedia loaded (audio.peaks, ui.media_lazy;
# verified 2026-09-04). dbus's message loader queues an incoming message inside
# _dbus_message_loader_queue_messages, reached from libQt6DBus through a
# QSocketNotifier while the app is still starting; the buffer belongs to the
# connection, and the connection is a process-lifetime singleton dbus never
# tears down. This is the same family of allocations the four ASan-built engine
# suites cite when they set detect_leaks=0 (there, reached from the Vulkan
# loader's Mesa device-select layer instead of from Qt DBus).
leak:libdbus-1.so

# libdrm via FFmpeg — 87 bytes in 4 allocations, in the two suites that let Qt
# Multimedia probe hardware decoders (audio.peaks, ui.media_lazy; verified
# 2026-09-04). libavutil calls drmGetVersion() while enumerating DRM render
# nodes and never calls drmFreeVersion(); the struct plus its three strdup'd
# strings are the whole leak. One per device probe, not per file decoded.
leak:drmGetVersion

# glibc's pthread_cond 2.0-ABI compat shim — 48 bytes, one allocation, in
# ui.media_lazy on about two runs in three (verified 2026-09-04; it appears only
# when FFmpeg finds a hardware decoder to probe). A codec plugin libavcodec
# dlopen()s still calls the pre-2.3.2 pthread_cond_broadcast symbol;
# old_pthread_cond_broadcast.c allocates the new-ABI condition variable behind
# it and keeps it in a table glibc never frees, by design. Nothing we compile
# can reach the 2.0 symbol — modern glibc headers only bind the current one —
# so this can never hide a leak of ours. One per condvar in that plugin.
leak:__pthread_cond_broadcast_2_0
